  "nameStory": "",
  "description": "Leccinum holopus, commonly called the Ghost Bolete, is a distinctive fungus in the bolete family characterized by its pale, ghostly appearance. This mushroom is found in New England and other temperate regions, typically fruiting in late summer and fall.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Pale coloration",
      "description": "The entire fruiting body is whitish to pale gray, giving it a ghostly appearance that distinguishes it from most other boletes",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Pores and stem structure",
      "description": "Features small yellow pores that may bruise slightly, with a sturdy white stem that may have subtle grayish fibrils",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Flesh characteristics",
      "description": "The flesh is white and does not change color significantly when cut, and lacks the red or orange tones seen in related species",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "Ghost Boletes fruit in mixed deciduous and coniferous forests of New England, often appearing in mossy areas and near birch or aspen trees during late summer and early autumn.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "",
    "summer": "",
    "fall": "",
    "winter": ""
  },
  "funFact": "The Ghost Bolete's pale coloration is unusual among Leccinum species, most of which display vibrant orange or reddish caps, making this bolete a striking and memorable find for mushroom foragers and mycologists.",
